      $VER: Rush Hour, v1.50 History File (15-May-98) by Dotoran!
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

- v1.50 - May 14, 1998

  - BugFix: The ESC key alias for exiting the game did NOT work!

  - While playing the game on my IBM computer, I noted that the graphics
    really looked screwed up. Instead of being SOLID colored cars with
    black lettering, they displayed as colored outlines with lettering
    the same as the color of the car. In some instances, there were even
    vehicles with lettering you couldn't even SEE! So, I've created a NEW
    graphics set internally in ADDITION TO the existing one. You can use
    the `U' key to USE the other Graphics. (It's a toggle, so pressing `U'
    again will return you to the original graphics).

  - Being as yet ANOTHER command was added to the ever expanding list, this
    made the scrolling Help Text even LONGER. So... There is now a Quick
    Help Text which will scroll initially by default. The `1' and `2' keys
    can be used to switch back and forth between the Quick Help and the
    Extended Help scroll texts!

  - I also noted while playing the game on an IBM that the CopyRight symbol
    used to denote Binary Arts' legal status was not being displayed as the
    actual (c) symbol, but was using another symbol instead. It should NOW
    display as a REAL copyright symbol on BOTH platforms!

  - While watching a user play the game on my system, I noted that they
    were being given the MORE prompt a lot throughout the game. The game
    now disables the More? prompt(if it was enabled to start with) when
    entering the game. When the game is Stopped, completely Solved, or
    exited due to an error condition, the More? mode is set back to it's
    previous state(dependent on how the user had it set in the first place).

  - While playing around with the ZIP mode, I discovered that whenever you
    exited the game, the location you were last at was what was saved to the
    data file. This is correct behavior, however if you solved the puzzles
    sequentially and made it to, say, Level 2, Card 5, then chose to ZIP back
    to Level 1, card 8 and then exited the game, when you went back into the
    game, you'd find yourself at Level 1, card 8, instead of Level 2, Card 5,
    which was REALLY where you left off. For this reason, I decided to change
    the way this saving was being done. Now, the position is ONLY saved if you
    got to that card/level SEQUENTIALLY, meaning that if you wanted Level 2,
    Card 5 stored in your file, you will have to have solved all Level 1 puzzles
    as well as the first 4 cards from Level 2, etc. You can still use the ZIP
    command to try out ANY puzzle you'd like, but to receive "credit" for it
    in the eyes of the program, you'll have to have done it sequentially.

  - While rewriting the above code, it occurred to me that it'd be a royal
    pain in the backside if the user had to constantly set their graphics set,
    scroll speed and/or scroll enabled status each time they entered the game.
    So, in addition to the above change, the NEW data file also stores the
    users' Scroll Enable/Disable status, the Scrolling Speed Value, as well as
    which Graphics Set they chose to use. Now, the user simply has to enter the
    game, set the different settings to their preference, then exit the game and
    never have to worry about it again!

  - It was because of the addition of the above two points that those SysOps
    who chose to UPDATE from a previous version had to DELETE their existing
    data file. This was because it was impossible to tell whether the user's
    last saved position had actually been gotten the "correct" way, as outlined
    two points above! ;-)

  - Lastly, I did some consolidation to the coding to tighten it up a bit, which
    will result in the game loading and executing a bit faster. It'll be a bit
    harder for those interested in looking at the code, however I put a liberal
    amount of commenting throughout the file, which should prove helpful! ;-)

- v1.03 - May 12, 1998 (Not sure if I officially released this version?!?)

  - Look above, as I can't recall what made it into this version, and what
    was considered NEW for the 1.50 version! ;-)

- v1.02 - May 9, 1998

  - Added a logentry line for logging purposes.

  - Added a disable/enable command for the scrolling using the `*' key.

  - At times, the color of the scroll text would inherit the wrong color.

  - The disabled scrolling stays in effect throughout the current puzzle,
    however when you complete a puzzle and proceed to the next puzzle, the
    scrolling is re-enabled, so that the Hi Scores text can be scrolled.

  - Some changes needed to be made to the ANSI-sequence capture routines,
    so that if a user mistakenly used a cursor arrow key when one was not
    needed(as is the case when movement in only one direction exists) won't
    be mistakenly taken as an attempt to move piece A, B, C, or D.

- v1.01 - May 5, 1998

  - When dual vertical movements were possible, the `U' key would not work,
    however the UP arrow, the `8', and the Shift-U (capital) worked.

- v1.00 - May 3, 1998

  - After initial ALPHA Testing here.

